      "ass:variance-nondegeneracy",
      "lem:tropp-matrix-freedman"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Identification gives \\(E_P[E_{ij}\\mid\\mathbb G_{o(i,j)-1}]=0\\).  The loadings and \\(s_g\\) are deterministic, so multiplication by \\(\\sqrt N g_{ij}/s_g\\) proves the vector martingale-difference assertion.  Variance nondegeneracy, dense loadings, and the increment bound from identification give\n\\[\n |X_{k,g}|\n \\le\\frac{\\sqrt N(C_\\infty/N)(3B/q)}{\\sqrt{c_s}B/\\sqrt N}\n =\\frac{3C_\\infty}{q\\sqrt{c_s}}.\n\\]\nThus \\(N^{-1}\\sum_kE_P\\lVert X_k\\rVert_\\infty^3=O(1)\\), uniformly over the model class; since \\(N\\to\\infty\\), it is at most \\(N^{1/8}\\) eventually.  Bounded aspect ratio and \\(d\\le n^\\kappa\\) give\n\\[\n \\frac{\\log^{7/2}d}{N^{1/4}}\n \\le C\\frac{\\log^{7/2}(en)}{\\sqrt n}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\n\nFor each pair \\((g,g')\\), set\n\\[\n D_{k,gg'}=X_{k,g}X_{k,g'}-\n E_P[X_{k,g}X_{k,g'}\\mid\\mathbb G_{k-1}].\n\\]\nThese are scalar martingale differences bounded by a fixed constant, with predictable quadratic variation at most a fixed multiple of \\(N\\).  The scalar case of the cited Freedman inequality and a union bound over at most \\(d^2\\) pairs give\n\\[\n \\max_{g,g'}\\left|N^{-1}\\sum_kD_{k,gg'}\\right|\n =O_P\\!\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(ed)}N}+\\frac{\\log(ed)}N\\right)\n =O_P\\!\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(ed)}N}\\right).\n\\]\nFinally,\n\\[\n N^{-1/2}\\log^{1/2}(ed)\n =o\\{N^{-1/8}\\log^{-2}(eNd)\\}\n\\]\nunder polynomial growth.  This proves precisely the oracle primitive claims.  None of these calculations compares \\(\\widehat e_{ij}\\) with \\(E_{ij}\\); such a comparison cannot be obtained merely by calling a rank approximation deterministic when the fit contains observations later than the held-out cell.",
